t they don't  tend to relapse  and when there's minor fluctuations one  can make  dose adjustments in their medication  actually  we never change the dose of the dbs over  years once we get an effective  dose so the answer is yes  thank you helen our next question is  coming in  for kareem so kareem since you are  studying uh patients with epilepsy do  you make or see  any distinction between the  physiological  and pathological ripples that you may  actually be recording from the  intracranial eeg  so thank you for the question i think  it's a very good question um  as you noted we are recording uh from  patients who do have epilepsy and so in  those patients we do  often see uh pathological activity both  intrical discharges as well as  pathological ripples people have studied  these ripples  uh these pathological ripples and there  has been some suggestions literature  that they actually  are detrimental rather than beneficial  for cognition  uh we in our work generally when we are  thinking about normal memory retrieval  we try as best as possible to remove  pathological  events from our data analysis so we try  to exclude electrodes that are involved  in badlas productivity  as well as individual trials that  exhibit what we find  is in the ripples that we capture we see  these sequences of spiking activity that  are actually  relevant for memory retrieval we  hypothesize but we haven't actually  shown this yet but we plan to do this we  hypothesize that if we were to look at  the pathological ripples  what we expect to see are sequences of  psychiatry that are actually not  uh organized in a consistent manner or  that may not be organized in a way that  is beneficial for memory  again we haven't really done this but  that would be sort of our working  hypothesis at this point  thanks green the next question is for  ted  do individual differences in functional  topography relate in any way to  underlying anatomical or structural  differences  across individuals