ly  imagining and attempting to perform  these movements so they're not getting  any actual  sensory feedback during this task and so  what we're measuring here is really  you know kind of that forward model  command  to the to the limb i think um you know  it's hard to say because these  participants do have traumatic spinal  cord injury and in the past have  actually grasped with their own hands  and so you know it may be that they have  this  efferent communication or afferent  communication going back and forth  between some other  metasensory and motor cortex that has  driven these memories but right now  we're seeing these responses in the  absence of any actual  sensory feedback  thank you jen um the next question i  have is for helen  did you find anything in your  data so far that you did not expect  well i think we had i mean you always  find things in your data you don't  expect  but i think that what has  been helpful for us is to actually let  the data tell us what we should see  rather than actually thinking we knew  what we were going to see  and this switch between  a signal that happened early actually  transitioned and actually morphed into  a different kind of signal late and the  appreciation  that the recovery wasn't linear neither  at  the clinical level but neither at the  brain level  and i think it really gives us pause out  about how to think about what these  devices do do they reset the brain and  the rest is  all rehab are we looking at  acute changes and then plasticity  changes i think we have to have  different models  and that's really something we have to  pay more attention to now  great question is for  kareem again um which micro  micro electrodes excuse me array did you  use  and where do you think these units are  located within the brain  so the microelectrode array that we used  was the  uh what's locally known as the utah  microscope it's an array of 96  microelectrodes arranged in a four by  four millimeter grid  so each microelectrode is based from its  neighbor by approximately 400 microns  uh we place these electrodes in the vast  majority of cases  in the middle temporal gyrus of the  anterior temporal lobe so the lateral  temporal cortex  just because that's the area that we  have access to when we're performing our  initial surgery for placement of the  clinical contacts  again largely we are placing these  microelectrodes in this region because  this is an area that we anticipate that  we are going to resect and so as best  as possible we try to put these arrays  in areas of the brain that we think is  going to be removed so it doesn't have  any long-term consequences  uh the electrodes themselves each extend  approximately a millimeter  into the cortex so we don't know for  sure exactly which  layer they are reporting from but we  think that it's approximately layer  three or layer four neurons in that part  of the lateral temporal cortex  thanks cream um the next question i have  here  is for gen uh with your decoding  approach would someone be able to change  the amount of force  that they are applying to an object  thanks for the question so yeah we've  actually looked at this a little bit  because the way that the task  is designed that i presented was the  hand is starts open  at least they visually see that and then  it closes and starts to apply the force  and we initially measured that  transient response during that closing  phase so we weren't sure  if that was related to you the kinematic  movement of the hand  but actually even during an isometric  grasp when you're holding on to the  object  if the user wants to increase or  decrease that force we see that they  enter back into that same  transient dimension and so we can use a  single decoder  to either apply that initial force or  change it  as you as you go along and so it's we're  not really switching back and forth but  we're looking at the probability  of whether you're in that transient  state or not and that tends to be high  again if you want to change your force  great thank you jen and we are going to  wrap up the  question and answers with um that last  answer from jen and again i want to  thank all of our speakers we really  appreciate  you uh taking the time to work through  the pre-recording and joining us today  for the live question to answer  it's wonderful um that we can all do  this virtually  in this very sort of different time that  we're in right now  and thank you to the audience for your  participation and your attention  and i hope everyone stays well and  enjoyed the symposium thank you  you

