hello  my name is brooks gross my colleague and  co-organizer  dr carrie ashmont and i are program  officers at the national institute of  neurological disorders and stroke  we actively participate in the brain  initiative and each manage portfolios of  translational device grants that include  brain-funded projects  we are delighted to welcome you to this  brand initiative scientific symposium  titled advances in human neuroscience  this session will highlight recent  advances made towards understanding the  human nervous system  speakers will also present  considerations they encounter and there  were epic considerations they encounter  in their work  if you have any questions for the  speakers please type them in the q  a box during the presentations please  address your questions  to a specific speaker if possible we  will be curating your questions as they  come  in and we'll do our best to answer as  many questions as possible during the  live q a  session at the end of the symposium now  i would like to introduce our first  speaker  dr jen collinger a research biomedical  engineer at the va r d center of  excellence  and an assistant professor in the  department of physical medicine and  rehabilitation at the university of  pittsburgh  all right well thank you to brooks and  kerry for inviting me here to talk about  some of the work that we've been doing  at the university of pittsburgh  using a neuroscience-based approach to  try to restore grasp function  after spinal cord injury so now as many  of you  likely know after spinal cord injury  your level of function really depends on  the level of the injury itself  and we're interested in trying to  restore upper limb function to people  with tetraplegia or quadriplegia  who have an injury at the cervical  spinal cord and so this study that was  done in 2004  demonstrated quite clearly that people  with quadriplegia really prioritize  restoration of arm and hand function  over all other functional recovery  priorities  and so our research group is trying to  use brain computer interfaces or bcis  to tap into a brain activity that  remains intact after injury  to try to bridge that injured spinal  cord and restore movement  this slide here shows you know sort of  an overview of our approach  so we're using what we call a  bi-directional bci  where it's it's a circular loop that  we can record activity from the brain so  shown in blue there is the motor cortex  and there's two microelectrode arrays  that are implanted into motor cortex  in our study you can see an image of one  off to the top right of the slide  that actually lets us record from single  neurons or small groups of neurons in  the brain  we can record and look at the patterns  of activity and turn that into a control  signal for  a robotic arm and at the same time  we can record from sensors in that  robotic arm  and turn that into stimulation patterns  for electrode arrays that are implanted  into somatosensory cortex to try to  restore the sense of touch  and so we know a lot about how motor  cortex is involved in controlling  reaching movements  and grasping movements from you know  decades of work that have been done in  non-human primates  and so um you know we've been doing a  lot of work to try to restore reaching  and simple grasping activities  and now have the ability to provide the  sense of touch to somebody with spinal  cord injury  where we can actually generate  sensations that feel like they're coming  from the person's fingers  and we can grade the intensity of those  sensations  this video will give you an idea of the  typical level of performance that we're  able to achieve  using the bci to control this robotic  arm so the participant is in control of  the endpoint velocity of the robotic arm  moving it around in three-dimensional  space as well as three-dimensional  orientation of the wrist  and opening and closing all of the  fingers as a group and so here they're  using the vci to perform the action  research arm test it's a test of upper  limb function  where the goal of the task is to pick up  the object and place it on a raised  surface  and so you can see that the bci is  really enabling a fairly natural and  smooth control over the robotic arm  and with smartest sensory feedback this  performance only improves  so using population level recordings  from motor cortex  we can actually restore simultaneous and  continuous control over all of these  degrees of freedom  you know to restore fairly natural  looking reaching and grasping movement  so what we're interested in now now that  we have the ability to restore some  reaching movements as well as sensation  is trying to focus on control of the  hand  and so we know that the ability to  regulate your grasped force is critical  for many activities of daily living you  know something adventurous  like rock climbing or playing tennis um  but even very simple things like  holding on to your briefcase and making  sure you don't drop it or shaking hands  or  you know interacting with fragile  objects such as  cracking an egg and so we really want to  try to understand  as much as we can how motor cortex is  involved in controlling grasped force in  order to  take a biometic approach to restoring  this function for somebody with spinal  cord injury  and so today i'm going to talk about  work that was done by brian zaclava  a postdoc in my laboratory who really  wanted to understand how grass force was  controlled across a variety of contexts  so he you know within the context of our  study  had the participant tried to perform  these different actions so on the left  you see an image of a virtual  environment  where the task is to observe and attempt  to  control that that virtual hand to grasp  an  object and they were asked to grasp it  with either a soft or firm force  the next iteration of that task we call  the grasp and 1d  carry is that there's a cylinder that  appears in the workspace either on the  left or right side  the user is instructed to think about  grasping it with a soft or firm force  and then move it to the other target so  it's very simple you either move left or  right depending on where you start  on the right hand side you can see a  virtual environment where the task now  is to grasp  and 3d carry and so what that means is  that the targets could appear  at select locations within the  three-dimensional workspace and so there  was more uncertainty and complexity  as to the reaching movement that would  be required after the user  grasped that object and so again this  work was happening in the context of our  brain computer interface studies  so brian was looking at neural  recordings from motor cortex of a person  who had a chronic spinal cord injury at  the c5 c6  level you know and the reason he wanted  to look at this activity across a  variety of contexts was because  there'd been some non-human primate work  it really suggested that motor cortex  firing was context dependent  and specifically what i'm referring to  for the study referenced here at  raymond was that the firing rates were  modulated based on the range of forces  that the monkeys were asked to achieve  so two different force levels might have  a different firing rate  when performed in a two target task as  opposed to a three target task  so what brian did was we had the  recordings like i mentioned we can  compute the firing rate for all of the  channels across time  and then use dimensionality reduction to  try to look at the overall population  level activity  which is something that's commonly being  done in this field right now so here  we're just looking at the first three  principal components of the population  activity  when the participant was attempting  either a high force  shown in dark purple or a low force  grasp shown in light purple  and so you can see that they have you  know a fairly stereotyped pattern  that looks very similar except they're  different in magnitude  and so what brian did is um found two  different axes that described two  different types of responses that  contained information about force  and so if we look at the projection of  this activity onto these two different  axes  we see what we call a transient mode so  the projection along that transient axis  shows that there's a peak in activity  kind of early on in the trial right at  the time of force onset so that dash  line is showing the computer controlled  force that the user was attempting to  to generate and you can see that there's  a large response for the large force  target in dark red and a small response  for the low force target in the lighter  red  and if we look at the projection onto  the other axis which we call the tonic  axis  you can see that after that initial  force onset while the force is being  held  but there's again a modulated activity  based on the high force or low force  target shown in dark or light blue  and so we consistently see these  different modes of activity  in our population level recordings  now what does it look like across the  different tasks well this is the data  that i just showed you  i'm only going to show the high force  target data right now but again we're  looking at the  magnitude of the projection of the  neural activity  onto this transient and red and tonic  axis  if we look at this activity for the  grasp plus the one-dimensional carry  you know again during that initial  application of the grasp  movement at the beginning there you can  see that there's a transient  response in the population shown in red  that looks very similar to the grasp  only task  but during the carry phase you know we  do see that there's a tonic response but  it's a little bit lower in magnitude as  a  compared to the grasp only task  and then when we look at the  three-dimensional carry task again we  see  a transient response initially as the  grasp is applied  but during the carry phase throughout  the three-dimensional workspace  that tonic response is reduced in  magnitude  and we can quantify this by looking at  the ratio of the peak magnitude  in the tonic dimension as compared to  the transient dimension  what we found is that as the task  complexity increased so the  number of degrees of freedom that needed  to be controlled or the number of  targets that were available  this ratio decreased and so initially  during the grasp only case  the ratio was approximately one where  the tonic and transient components had a  similar magnitude  but that was decreased as the kinematic  complexity was increased  and so what this meant for designing a  new grass force decoder is that we seem  to have very stable and reliable  information  during that transient phase and no  matter what if you're applying a high  grasp force  the population response looked similar  during that transient phase  but that tonic grass force information  was unreliable because it was context  dependent  and so brian came up with the idea to  design a decoder that preferentially  weighted this transient force  information  and so he was using a standard kalman  filter to control  grasp force in our bci where the force  output is really a measure of  the incoming neural activity that is  transformed into force  as well as a measure of the the force of  the previous time step  and so the kalman filter gain really  regulates how much you weight  incoming information versus historical  information  and so brian did was insert another  module in here where we can actually  detect what is the probability that the  user is  in this transient state of actively  trying to train change their grasp force  and if that probability is high we know  that they want to  you know actively change and modulate  the amount of force that they're  applying so we should probably pay more  attention to the incoming neural  information  and less so to the historical  information you know but conversely if  they're not in a transient state if  they're in that tonic state  we know that they probably want to just  keep doing what they're doing and  maintain that same grasp force  so in order to implement this this is  just one example of a task  where the user was asked to pick up an  object and move it back and forth across  the table as many times as they could  without dropping it  using a standard kalman filter approach  you can see that they were able to  generate  different forces for a high and low  force target there in dark and light  green  but they were dropping the object pretty  consistently after four or five seconds  with the transient weighted decoder they  could maintain that performance for much  longer regularly on the order of 30  seconds  so this is something that we're  continuing to develop and quantify  for a variety of tasks you know but i  think what  brian has shown so far is that the  cortical control of grasp  has both these transient tonic  components and  the tonic grass force information is  really context dependent and depends on  the complexity of the task  so we're trying to use these  biologically inspired decoding  approaches to improve  performance and here we've shown that it  can enable prolonged grasping and  transport  there's a lot of work to do still to  evaluate how this actually  translates to functional performance on  tasks just providing some other sensory  feedback  improve or change the ability to to  regulate grasp force  and we want to increase the kinematic  complexity of the hand so really moving  towards dexterous movements  so one last um closing thought i was  asked to talk about implications for  small end studies so with a low number  of participants  you know i think there are a lot of  opportunities we get to work with  participants for  many years who come in multiple times  per week and they really provide unique  insights into the studies it also allows  us to really maximize the benefit and  risk  ratio with each participant because we  can sort of make big leaps in device  development or science between  participants  you know but of course there are  challenges related to generalizability  statistical power and trying to  translate this technology for a small  market and so i'd be happy to  talk about this during during the q a  session  but i'd like to thank all of the  collaborators who've really contributed  to this project as well as the  funding from nih and darpa that has  allowed us to do this work  of course i have to thank our study  participants without whom this wouldn't  be possible  so thank you for your attention and i'd  like to uh now just introduce our next  speaker  kareem zaghool  so thank you for inviting us to present  our work my name is kareem zaglul and  i'm an investigator in the intramural  program here at the national institutes  of health and our lab is primarily  focused on understanding the neural  mechanisms that underlie human episodic  memory formation  and so what i wanted to say was describe  a recent study that we just completed  examining how sequences of spiking  activity are  replayed in the cortex when individuals  retrieve memories  now the motivation for this work and one  of the key pieces of evidence that has  shaped how we think about memory emerges  from the literature on spatial  navigation and rodents  this is a schematic summary of that  literature and what we can see  are the presence of what are known as  place cells these are individual neurons  that respond to the locations that a  rodent is in when they are traversing a  spatial environment  such that when a rodent is moving along  the track what you'll see is a sequence  of firing of those place cells  now remarkably what happens when the  mouse is asleep and at rest and  perhaps consolidating their memory or at  rest and may be thinking about the track  is that we see a replay of these  sequences  uh and for many years it has been  inferred that these replay of spiking  activity must have something to do with  memory  yeah we haven't had such direct evidence  that spike replay plays an important  role in human memory retrieval  now we also know is that these replay  events also happen in the context of  this other physiological signal  known as a ripple which is a fast  oscillation in the field potential  recordings from the medial temporal  lobes in the rodent  brain so i'm going to show you today is  a story that uh  is comprised of four parts first i'm  going to show you that we can record  ripples in the human cortex and the  reflect burst of spiking activity  i'm going to show you that these spikes  are ordered in a specific temporal order  or sequence  i'm going to show you that when you  retrieve memories you actually replay  these  spike sequences and the extent to which  we see such replay is actually enhanced  when we see ripples  in the medial temporal lobe the  behavioral paradigm that we use for this  task is  what's known as a verbal paired  associates memory task so in this task  we present our subjects with pairs of  words we instruct them to form these  novel associations  they solve some simple math problems as  a way of distraction and then  subsequently during testing we cue them  with one word from each pair  and their job is to remember what the  associated word is and this paradigm  gives us an important advantage because  it gives us direct control over what  memories they're trying to encode and  importantly what memory they're trying  to retrieve  and when they're trying to retrieve it  and so we can see what cycling activity  looks like during encoding and then  during retrieval  so if we take a look at our recordings  that we capture from our clinical  context we can identify these ripples  uh this is our three electrodes the  middle temporal gyrus and the cortex  and electrode and medial temporal lobe  we've previously shown that the presence  of these ripples are linked with memory  formation  and now what we're going to do is we're  going to ask what happens to underlying  spiking activity  and to do this we actually implant these  microelectrode arrays  in our in our human participants who are  getting  clinical implants for seizure monitoring  this gives us an opportunity to record  activity both at this larger spatial  scale as well as at the smaller spatial  scale  we primarily place these arrays in the  anterior temporal lobe because this is a  region that we anticipate we're going to  resect a subsequent surgery  and a region that we think is involved  in their seizure activity and what we  find is that every time we see a ripple  event we see  a clear burst of spiking activity from  multiple neurons that are required by  this microelectrode array  and even within the array itself if we  look at the individual channels we can  see that each channel actually exhibits  this small micro ripple and the extent  which we see a ripple actually  correlates with  uh the spiking activity on that channel  okay so we've now seen that the ripples  are associated with these bursts of  spiking activity so now let's see what  happens within these bursts so what i'm  showing you here  is a single example of a burst of  spiking activity uh with the firing rate  of each individual neuron shot on the  right  and what we can do is we can actually  reorder these neurons so we know which  one fires first and we color that blue  and which one fires  last within that burst and so now if we  look at a single trial in our experiment  what we see is that every trial  involves multiple bursts of spiking  activity  and what we find is that the order of  spiking within the  first burst is actually preserved across  all the subsequent bursts so the neuron  that fired first  in that first burst tenth of fire later  in the susquehanna burst the neurons of  fire later the yellow neurons in that  first burst tend to fire  later subsequent bursts so now we can  look at what the sequence looks like  in one trial so in this trial the  subject is studying the word pair steam  and seal we can see that the blue  neurons fire earlier and the yellow  neurons fire later  and now we can ask what the same neurons  are doing in a second different trial  and what we find  is that when we observe a burst event in  that second trial  the neurons have actually changed their  order so the early neurons who fired  first  in the first trial tend to fire later in  the second trial then the runs will fire  later in that first trial tend to fire  earlier in that second trial  and now we can actually quantify the  extent to which sequences are similar to  one another using a metric known as a  matching index we can use any metric and  it gives the same result  and so what we're going to ask is how  similar the sequences are for  all the births within a given trial  compared to the first across different  trials  and what we find is that for the correct  encoding trials there's significantly  more similarity  within a trial than across different  trials and this is not true for the  incorrect trials so this suggests that  when you're correctly encoding an item  into memory  it actually involves a very specific  sequences of spiking activity  and so now we want to know what happens  during memory retrieval and so what i'm  showing you here is an example  of a trial during encoding where they  are studying the word pair crow and jeep  we can see a sequence of spiking  activity  as we said earlier if we look across the  entire trial we see multiple bursts of  activity that all have the same sequence  and now during retrieval we give them  the word jeep and they say the word crow  and what we find  is that there are also bursts of  activity that emerge during retrieval  and they have actually a very similar  pattern of spiking sequence  to the sequences that were present  during encoding and in fact retrieval  also involves multiple bursts of  fighting activity so  here the word