or system on the left hand of that  violin plot  so we've basically replicated uh  findings for an adult so far and shown  that there's a lot of variability in the  functional topography and the  variability is maximal in higher order  association cortex  especially that's the the parts of the  networks that are responsible for  executive function so next though what  we want to understand  is how this functional topography  related to things we cared about like  executive function  and changed with age  so one first crack of the data we took  was to summarize this very complex data  in a very simple way so we derived a  summary measure what we call the total  network representation  the total network representation is  literally the sum of the weights  of each functional network over the  entire cortex  it basically summarizes how much real  estate  each individualized network takes up on  a given person's cortex  now i want to one important point is  this is all done in normalized space  so we're not aliasing in individual  differences  and cortical surface area so this  basically says okay this total network  representation says  how much a given network is uh taking up  on  the cortex of a given individual and  what we found  was that this total network  representation doesn't change  much over the age range that we study  between 8 and 22. it's not like these  networks  are expanding or contracting  dramatically over the adolescent period  however we did find that total network  net total network representation  was associated with executive function  while controlling for h  so in fact what we found is that the  networks that were responsible for  executive function  like here on the left-hand panel the two  frontal two of the frontal parietal  networks  individuals who have more total network  representation of those executive  networks  perform better on executive tasks  so if they have more real estate devoted  to the executive networks they're  performing better at executive function  so this is a very very simple measure as  i said next we wanted to look at the  overall  complex multivariate pattern of  functional topography  and we found looking at the overall high  dimensional data  that we could predict executive function  with a reasonable degree of accuracy and  completely unseen data  to do this we use split half ridge  regression with nested parameter tuning  and what we found is that we can predict  executive function in the second half of  the data the model hadn't seen  and vice versa so we see prediction  correlations of 0.46 complete out of  sample testing  this is well beyond the precision of a  permutation testing procedure  and we were actually pretty pleased with  this this is a higher level prediction  we often see from functional mri  and shows that this functional this  pattern of functional topography  has important implications for behavior  however the next step we wanted to see  whether this whether again  this pattern of functional topography  was related to age and previously  in the when we were looking at the total  network representation we saw no  relationship  and so somewhat to our surprise  we saw that actually we could predict an  individual child's age with an even  higher degree of accuracy than we could  predict their executive function  again in complete out-of-sample testing  here we could predict an individual's  uh child's age within a two-year margin  of error  in a correlation of around 0.66 in in  out-of-sample testing  so that was somewhat surprising to us  because we know we know from the total  network representation these networks  aren't  aren't expanding or contracting much  over development so what could be  driving this effect so we took  so as i should did a number of analyses  try to investigate this  and what he found was that the features  that were driving the model  to be able to decode an unseen  individual's age  were present in association cortex and  the same association cortex in fact  that is most variable across individuals  so the association cortex topography  that varies the most among kids  also is what allows us to predict their  age  we would dug a little further and i  think that all the details are in the  paper and  what seems to be going on here is that  association network  topography at the edges of the network  is being refined over development  even though the gross amount of area  that they're taking up isn't expanding  or contracting  so as a final step  we want to understand how this network  variability which is related to age and  predicts executive function is  constrained by fundamental properties of  brain organization  so here what we did is we tried to  understand the relationship between  network variability  and fundamental properties like  evolutionary expansion  cortical myelin content and cerebral  blood flow  and to do this we used an extremely  conservative spatial permutation  testing procedure developed by iron  alexander block and we  plotted the network variability here on  the y axis of each of these plots  versus evolutionary expansion versus  cortical myelin content and versus  means cerebral blood flow what you can  see is that  these highly variable networks also  are have a high level of evolutionary  expansion have a low  cortical myelin content and elevated  cerebral blood flow  so i'm going to summarize these results  and try to put them together for you so  first we found that in adults functional  network topography  is highest in association networks and  youth  the spatial layout of these functional  networks varies a lot across individuals  just like we've seen adults  and the mo these networks tend to be  most variable in higher order  association cortex we look across the hp  and studied we found that kids who were  better at executive functioning  had the greater total network  representation of the  networks that are devoted executive  function like the front of parietal  network  furthermore we found that this  complex uh functional topography of the  species of association networks allowed  us to predict the age of individuals  and the executive performance of  individuals in unseen data  using ridge regression in unseen data  finally the cortex that allowed us to  breed  to predict age and executive function  that is also the most  variable in individuals we found is  though  is the same cortex that is that is  expanded through evolution  is lightly myelinated and receives  disproportionate through blood flow  if you put all this together the results  are remarkably consistent with  a model developed by fenugreene and  randy buckner  whereby in evolution association  networks become untethered  from rigid hardwired developmental  programming and  the thought is that this loss of  tethering and lack of hard wiring allows  for greater adaptability in development  but also comes at higher metabolic costs  and also potential  vulnerability to neuropsychiatric  syndromes  so this work has motivated a lot of  stuff we're working on now  as well as things we're planning this  includes relating it directly  to psychopathology going from cognitive  function to  clinical uh symptoms and so currently  she was working on a paper where  he uses multiview machine learning  techniques to link this  diversity of functional topography to  different measures of psychopathology in  a data-driven way  we're currently working with mike millam  uh in the healthy brain  network data to try to replicate and  generalize these results and we're  planning  to look at how these individual specific  functional networks  evolve on a person-specific basis over  time  using data from the adolescent brain and  cognitive development project in  collaboration with damian fair  finally and perhaps most excitingly in  work funded by the brain initiative  our collaborators desmond oaths and  young fan are using these individual  specific networks to target tms  so i want to end there by thanking the  huge team that is required for this sort  of work in particular as i shukui  the postdoc who did literally all of the  work for this project i want to thank  raquel gur the overall  the overall pi of the pnc and our  collaboration our collaborators in  radiology and bioengineering young thon  and hong wing lee  who developed the non-negative matrix  factorization technique  lastly i want to thank our funding  sources in particular the brain  initiative  and nimh who's made all this research  possible as well as our study  participants and their families  thank you  thank you all so much for those  wonderful talks and i want to extend a  thank you also to the audience for your  patience with us as we work through some  of these technical challenges today  we're going to open it now for some live  question and  answers i'm going to start with chris  so chris for your ect amplitude dosing  you looked at sort of the trade-off  between clinical outcomes and um  and side effects and i'm wondering if  you can talk a little bit about  what you think might be a specific  amplitude for optimal clinical outcomes  based on the work that you've done and  the results that you've seen so far  sure thank you uh looking at the  trade-off  between the six seven and 800 milliamp  arms  when we did an older patient population  between 50 and 80 years of age  it looks like 700 represents a cut off  where the inflection point about  non-response shifts  to increased ect response  this was also consistent with the  cognitive side effects looking at  phonemic as well as category fluency  where the 700 milliamp does seem to have  less  cognitive impairment however there is  tremendous variability  some of the 700 milliamp subjects had an  e field specifically in the hippocampus  that mirrored the 600 milliamp arms so  prior to recommending a different  amplitude is retaining clinical care i  think we need to take a step back  and really try and develop a method for  improving the precision of ect dosing  basically what happens outside the skull  is constant but inside the skull is  highly variable and we really want to  flip that maybe have  individualized amplitudes to achieve the  same  endpoint about what's happening inside  the skull  methods to do that would be e field form  bct which would have  limited translational impact we would  really like to employ some of the work  of dr listenbee  with amplitude titration and come up  with a multiplier to where we can do  amplitude titration to standardize the  dose and then  incorporate a neuroplasticity multiplier  thank you chris the next question is for  helen mayberg do you think  that there is a change in receptability  to rehabilitation following the dbs  treatment and have any of the patients  seen changes  in their psychiatric treatment efficacy  pre or post  dbs um thank you  brooks that's an interesting question  and kind of  resonates with what we observe these  people by definition  have failed ect psychotherapy  and at least four medications and it's  remarkable  the absence almost of side effects to  really extreme doses of medications that  they're on  you know prior to entering the study  they can  attempt to do psychotherapy but it's  just ineffective  as they're not demented there's a  disconnect  between the thought and the training and  actually have it  hold and what we notice is that  even from the beginning they have  capacity  to take on more activities so  interventions  like behavioral activation can be  really the first course because they  really don't yet seem to have the  machinery  to engage in the kind of rehabilitation  strategies  that the full-blown cognition behavioral  therapy requires  but actually after six eight weeks  it seems to shift and we're really  interested to know  if our changes in the brain going from a  low beta to a high data might reflect  this  transition so yes they become more  responsive even years out they don't  tend to relapse  and when there's minor fluctuations one  can make  dose adjustments in their medication  actually  we never change the dose of the dbs over  years once we get an effective  dose so the answer is yes  thank you helen our next question is  coming in  for kareem so kareem since you are  studying uh patients with epilepsy do  you make or see  any distinction between the  physiological  and pathological ripples that you may  actually be recording from the  intracranial eeg  so thank you for the question i think  it's a very good question um  as you noted we are recording uh from  patients who do have epilepsy and so in  those patients we do  often see uh pathological activity both  intrical discharges as well as  pathological ripples people have studied  these ripples  uh these pathological ripples and there  has been some suggestions literature  that they actually  are detrimental rather than beneficial  for cognition  uh we in our work generally when we are  thinking about normal memory retrieval  we try as best as possible to remove  pathological  events from our data analysis so we try  to exclude electrodes that are involved  in badlas productivity  as well as individual trials that  exhibit what we find  is in the ripples that we capture we see  these sequences of spiking activity that  are actually  relevant for memory retrieval we  hypothesize but we haven't actually  shown this yet but we plan to do this we  hypothesize that if we were to look at  the pathological ripples  what we expect to see are sequences of  psychiatry that are actually not  uh organized in a consistent manner or  that may not be organized in a way that  is beneficial for memory  again we haven't really done this but  that would be sort of our working  hypothesis at this point  thanks green the next question is for  ted  do individual differences in functional  topography relate in any way to  underlying anatomical or structural  differences  across individuals or are they distinct  yeah that's an excellent question i  think uh one thing to  note at the beginning is there is a lot  of anatomic variability in the same  places that there's  differences in functional topography  however the tools that we've used for a  while  are much better at handling them so  modern image  registration techniques with deformable  registrations and service-based  registrations  can line up the structural brains quite  well and uh  so techniques have accounted for that  and we've been able to study that in the  past  and previously we've just ignored the  functional variability and assumed that  by lining up brain structure we're also  lining up  brain function and so this is a  different axis of variability the  structural variability that has been  previously ignored  and by not accounting for this we're  basically aliasing in the spatial  distribution  of our variability functional networks  to any measurement of connectivity  so i think they're both important  sources of variability uh just one  hadn't been well studied in the past  great and our last question for  um jen jen do you do the transient  and tonic components represent motor  intent  in the data that you are collecting or  do you think that they could be related  to the brain processing of sensory  events  yeah thanks that's a really interesting  question you know and i'm not sure that  we can really ever completely separate  motor intent from sensory feedback  because those are so tightly linked  together  i think the evidence that we have  suggest that these are primarily  representing  motor intent and i can tell you a few  reasons why one is  we are able to record from some areas in  somatosensory cortex and while we do see  that there is a  transient and tonic response it tends to  be more muted than what we're measuring  in motor cortex at least for this  particular task  but if you remember that you know we're  working with people with spinal cord  injury who are really  imagining and attempting to perform  these movements so they're not getting  any actual  sensory feedback during this task and so  what we're measuring here is really  you know kind of that forward model  command  to the to the limb i think um you know  it's hard to say because these  participants do have traumatic spinal  cord injury and in the past have  actually grasped with their own hands  and so you know it may be that they have  this  efferent communication or afferent  communication going back and forth  between some other  metasensory and motor cortex that has  driven these memories but right now  we're seeing these responses in the  absence of any actual  sensory feedback  thank you jen um the next question i  have is for helen  did you find anything in your  data so far that you did not expect  well i think we had i mean you always  find things in your data you don't  expect  but i think that what has  been helpful for us is to actually let  the data tell us what we should see  rather than actually thinking we knew  what we were going to see  and this switch between  a signal that happened early actually  transitioned and actually morphed into  a different kind of signal late and the  appreciation  that the recovery wasn't linear neither  at  the clinical level but neither at the  brain level  and i think it really gives us pause out  about how to think about what these  devices do do they reset the brain and  the rest is  all rehab are we looking at  acute changes and then plasticity  changes i think we have to have  different models  and that's really something we have to  pay more attention to now  great question is for  kareem again um which micro  micro electrodes excuse me array did you  use  and where do you think these units are  located within the brain  so the microelectrode array that we used  was the  uh what's locally known as the utah  microscope it's an array of 96  microelectrodes arranged in a four by  four millimeter grid  so each microelectrode is based from its  neighbor by approximately 400 microns  uh we place these electrodes in the vast  majority of cases  in the middle temporal gyrus of the  anterior temporal lobe so the lateral  temporal cortex  just because that's the area that we  have access to when we're performing our  initial surgery for placement of the  clinical contacts  again largely we are placing these  microelectrodes in this region because  this is an area that we anticipate that  we are going to resect and so as best  as possible we try to put these arrays  in areas of the brain that we think is  going to be removed so it doesn't have  any long-term consequences  uh the electrodes themselves each extend  approximately a millimeter  into the cortex so we don't know for  sure exactly which  layer they are reporting from but we  think that it's approximately layer  three or layer four neurons in that part  of the lateral temporal cortex  thanks cream um the next question i have  here  is for gen uh with your decoding  approach would someone be able to change  the amount of force  that they are applying to an object  thanks for the question so yeah we've  actually looked at this a little bit  because the way that the task  is designed that i presented was the  hand is starts open  at least they visually see that and then  it closes and starts to apply the force  and we initially measured that  transient response during that closing  phase so we weren't sure  if that was related to you the kinematic  movement of the hand  but actually even during an isometric  grasp when you're holding on to the  object  if the user wants to increase or  decrease that force we see that they  enter back into that same  transient dimension and so we can use a  single decoder  to either apply that initial force or  change it  as you as you go along and so it's we're  not really switching back and forth but  we're looking at the probability  of whether you're in that transient  state or not and that tends to be high  again if you want to change your force  great thank you jen and we are going to  wrap up the  question and answers with um that last  answer from jen and again i want to  thank all of our speakers we really  appreciate  you uh taking the time to work through  the pre-recording and joining us today  for the live question to answer  it's wonderful um that we can all do  this virtually  in this very sort of different time that  we're in right now  and thank you to the audience for your  participation and your attention  and i hope everyone stays well and  enjoyed the symposium thank you  you
 

